 {
  "char": "2",
  "code": "U+0032",
  "name": "Digit Two",
  "shape_diff": 0.464,
  "decision": "mix",
  "notes": "We may decide to change this later. Keep in mind the stroke width here as well, you can definitely see how it changes during the curve where CSL does not."
 },
 {
  "char": "3",
  "code": "U+0033",
  "name": "Digit Three",
  "shape_diff": 0.523,
  "notes": "Again what I think is important to take from CSL is the stroke width and curve. You can see if you compare the bottom curve of JBM against the top curve of CSL that CSL is slightly more squared off, so where the line starts to curve is a bit later than where it curves in JBM, giving it a more square look while retaining the curve. This is essential to making the chunks look good for other characters, though I'm not sure if it's necessary for this number.",
  "decision": "jbm"
 },
 {
  "char": "4",
  "code": "U+0034",
  "name": "Digit Four",
  "shape_diff": 0.617,
  "decision": "jbm"
 },
 {
  "char": "5",
  "code": "U+0035",
  "name": "Digit Five",
  "shape_diff": 0.318,
  "decision": "mix",
  "notes": "Stroke width, curvature"
 },
 {
  "char": "6",
  "code": "U+0036",
  "name": "Digit Six",
  "shape_diff": 0.475,
  "decision": "jbm",
  "notes": "Same issues as `9`, CSL can be mistaken for a `g` or `b` or `d` for dyslexic people like myself. The JBM `6` is better in this case, but again, the stroke width may need to be adjusted as well as adjusting the curve so it is more squared off, taking from CSL."
 },
 {
  "char": "7",
  "code": "U+0037",
  "name": "Digit Seven",
  "shape_diff": 0.331,
  "decision": "jbm"
 },
 {
  "char": "8",
  "code": "U+0038",
  "name": "Digit Eight",
  "shape_diff": 0.289,
  "notes": "Probably the biggest example of how if the curvature were more like CSL it would instantly look better. This is entering comic sans territory, but I don't think that this number would benefit from having chunks so I'm going with JBM. ",
  "decision": "mix"
 },
 {
  "char": "9",
  "code": "U+0039",
  "name": "Digit Nine",
  "shape_diff": 0.53,
  "decision": "jbm",
  "notes": "You can see here that the bottom right hand side of the number has a tapered stroke width, whereas the CSL one does not. This is actually sort of important to achieve the structured look of CSL. However, you can see that because of how the CSL 9 is designed, it actually could easily be confused for a lowercase `g`. We need some kind of middle ground where we don't have too much taper to keep the structured look."
 },
